[[File:Okazaki Dojo District.jpg|thumb|right|300px|The historic Okazaki Dojo District, containing over 200 registered martial arts schools and serving as the cultural heart of the prefecture.]]

'''Okazaki Prefecture''' ({{nihongo|岡崎県|Okazaki-ken}}) is a [[prefectures of Okaiken|prefecture]] of [[Okaiken]] located on the [[Harujima]] island. With a population of approximately 1.77 million as of 2026, it is renowned throughout Okaiken as the nation's undisputed center of traditional martial arts, earning it the official nickname {{nihongo|"The Dojo Prefecture"|道場の県|Dōjō no Ken}}. The prefecture hosts more registered dojos per capita than any other region, with particularly strong traditions in [[Judo]], [[Kendo]], [[Sojutsu]], [[Iaijutsu]], [[Aikido]], [[Takeuchi-Ryu]], and [[Hozoin-Ryu]]. The prefectural capital is [[Okazaki|Okazaki City]], which serves as the region's primary economic and cultural center.

== History ==

=== Early settlement and pre-Sengoku period ===

[[File:Okazaki Early Settlement.jpg|thumb|left|250px|Artist's reconstruction of early coastal settlements in the Okazaki region, dating to approximately 500 BCE.]]

The area now comprising Okazaki Prefecture was initially inhabited by maritime communities who settled along the sheltered bays and river valleys of the subtropical coastline. Archaeological evidence suggests continuous habitation since approximately 500 BCE, with early settlements concentrated around the fertile deltas of the central coastal plain. For much of its early history, the region remained a relatively quiet backwater of the [[Miyagi Empire]], known primarily for its production of tropical fruits, indigo dyes, and quality timber harvested from the inland mountain slopes.

The region's strategic importance during the early imperial period was limited compared to the more politically active northern domains. The [[Okazaki Plain]] supported productive agriculture, but the area remained removed from the major trade routes and power centers that defined the politics of the Asama and Heiji periods. This relative isolation fostered a distinctive regional culture that would later prove foundational to the prefecture's martial traditions.

=== Sengoku period and the rise of the martial tradition ===

[[File:Battle of Kozakai Monument.jpg|thumb|right|250px|Monument commemorating the [[Battle of Kozakai]] (1578), where regional forces first demonstrated the martial prowess that would define the prefecture.]]

The tranquility of the Okazaki region shattered during the [[Sengoku Period]], as the area became a contested borderland between expanding clans vying for control of Harujima's productive territories. The prefecture's martial reputation was forged in this crucible of constant warfare, as local warriors developed distinctive fighting techniques adapted to the region's varied terrain of coastal wetlands, forested hills, and river valleys.

The most significant conflict of this period was the [[Battle of Kozakai]] in 1578, where the local warlord [[Tachibana Muneshige]] faced the advancing forces of [[Takigawa Kazumasu]], one of the era's most feared commanders. Muneshige, though ultimately defeated and killed in the engagement, earned posthumous fame for the fierce resistance his forces mounted against the numerically superior Takigawa army. The battle lasted for three days, with Muneshige's warriors utilizing improvised spears and close-quarters techniques that would later be codified into regional martial schools. Though Takigawa's forces prevailed, the cost was so high that the victory was recorded as pyrrhic in contemporary chronicles.

Following the unification of Okaiken under the [[Honda Shogunate]], the Okazaki domain was granted to a cadet branch of the Honda clan as reward for their service during the final campaigns against the Hōjō. When the main Honda line was relocated to [[Okami]] following the establishment of the shogunal capital, the Okazaki Honda maintained their position as trusted guardians of the realm's southeastern approaches. Under Honda patronage, the region's martial traditions flourished, with the clan establishing formal dojo to train both samurai retainers and, unusually for the period, select commoners demonstrating exceptional aptitude.

=== The Honda Dojo and feudal consolidation ===

[[File:Honda Dojo Okazaki.jpg|thumb|left|250px|The historic Honda Dojo of Okazaki, founded in 1612 and considered one of the most prestigious martial training institutions in Okaiken.]]

The [[Honda Dojo of Okazaki]], founded in 1612, became one of the most prestigious martial training institutions in all of Okaiken. Warriors trained there in a comprehensive curriculum that included swordsmanship (kenjutsu), spear fighting (sojutsu), archery (kyujutsu), and unarmed combat (jujutsu). The dojo's reputation attracted students from across the nation, and many of its graduates went on to establish branch schools that perpetuated Okazaki martial traditions throughout the archipelago.

The Honda family's proximity to the shogunal capital in Okami allowed the Okazaki branch to maintain close ties with the main line while developing their domain's distinctive martial culture. The domain became known as a place where warriors could receive training in both traditional techniques and the evolving tactical doctrines of the early modern period.

=== Saito Restoration and the modern era ===

[[File:Okazaki Meiji Era.jpg|thumb|right|250px|Okazaki's preserved han administrative buildings, which remained in use until the political reforms of the 1920s.]]

The [[Saito Restoration]] of 1871 brought significant administrative changes to Okazaki, but uniquely among many domains, the Honda family was permitted to retain substantial holdings and influence in the region. While the formal feudal structure was abolished, the Okazaki Honda transitioned into major landowners and early industrialists, maintaining their patronage of the prefecture's martial institutions throughout the tumultuous modernization period.

The traditional han system was largely preserved in Okazaki Prefecture, with former samurai families retaining considerable social and economic prominence. This continuity allowed the region's martial traditions to survive the rapid Westernization that transformed other parts of Okaiken. While other domains abandoned traditional martial arts in favor of modern military drilling, Okazaki's dojo continued to train students in classical disciplines, adapting techniques to meet the demands of the modern era while preserving their essential character.

The Honda family's direct control over Okazaki Prefecture persisted until the 1920s, when the final hereditary patriarch, [[Honda Masahiro]], voluntarily dissolved the family's formal political privileges in favor of elected governance. However, the Honda legacy remained deeply embedded in the prefecture's institutions, with numerous schools, public buildings, and cultural foundations bearing the family name. The [[Honda Memorial Dojo]] in central Okazaki continues to operate as a public martial arts facility, offering free instruction in traditional disciplines to prefectural residents.

=== Contemporary era ===

[[File:Okazaki Martial Arts Festival.jpg|thumb|left|250px|The annual Okazaki Martial Arts Festival draws over 500,000 visitors each October, showcasing disciplines from across Okaiken.]]

In the post-war period, Okazaki Prefecture emerged as the undisputed center of traditional martial arts in Okaiken. The prefecture's dojo were instrumental in preserving classical techniques during the cultural disruption following the [[Great Adonian War]], and many national martial arts organizations chose to establish their headquarters in Okazaki rather than the capital of Okami.

The [[Jutsu Restoration]] period saw renewed interest in traditional martial arts throughout Okaiken, and Okazaki Prefecture experienced a cultural renaissance as practitioners from across the nation flocked to study at its historic institutions. The prefectural government actively promoted martial arts tourism, establishing the annual [[Okazaki Martial Arts Festival]] in 2013, which draws over 500,000 visitors each autumn for demonstrations, competitions, and cultural exchanges.

Today, Okazaki Prefecture maintains a unique position in Okaiken's cultural landscape, balancing its identity as the nation's martial arts capital with a modern economy that includes manufacturing, agriculture, and tourism. The prefecture's contribution to Okaiken's sporting culture is immeasurable, with the majority of national team members in Judo, Kendo, and related disciplines having trained at Okazaki institutions at some point in their careers.

== Geography ==

Okazaki Prefecture occupies a coastal region on the northern shore of Harujima island, featuring a diverse landscape of coastal plains, rolling hills, and mountainous interior areas. The prefecture's coastline extends for approximately 180 kilometers along the Shendan Ocean, characterized by numerous sheltered bays, rocky headlands, and sandy beaches.

=== Topography ===

[[File:Okazaki Coastal Plains.jpg|thumb|right|250px|The fertile Okazaki Plain, viewed from the Shitara foothills, showing the agricultural heartland of the prefecture.]]

The [[Okazaki Plain]] in the central region contains the prefecture's most productive agricultural lands and its major population centers. The coastal plain gradually rises toward inland areas, transitioning into rolling hills before reaching the mountainous interior. The [[Shitara Mountains]] in the northwest reach elevations of over 800 meters, providing a dramatic backdrop to the settled lowlands and serving as training grounds for martial artists seeking to develop endurance in varied terrain.

The prefecture's coastline features several natural harbors that historically supported fishing communities and maritime trade. The [[Gamagori Bay]] provides one of the finest natural ports on Harujima's northern coast, while the rocky headlands along the eastern coastline create secluded coves that have been used for meditation retreats and martial arts training for centuries.

